 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>New season, a new Kim Shui. The designer, who celebrated her 10-year anniversary in February, has a new lease on life. She not only has a new showroom space and a new PR team but also a revived sense of creativity. It comes after a great summer for the brand: Dua Lipa sported one of the designer’s signature butterfly chiffon qipao dresses that made its debut in spring 2020.</p><p>Shui is a natural performer. She’s no stranger to an insane show: Last season, she took people on a boat, and a few years ago, to the top of New York City, 94 stories above ground. This season, she did her first outdoor show a few blocks from her showroom. “The collection started with the varying layers of performance, and I was heavily inspired by Beijing opera thanks to my grandfather,” said the designer. “That’s why I wanted to do it on the streets of New York, because the runway is the performance.”</p><p>The collection started off with typical Shui pieces: Chinese-inspired designs that came in various sets. But she brought back a newer category, menswear, which she softly launched during the spring 2025 season. “I thought it would be interesting to have some men’s looks, because in Beijing opera they actually have a lot of guys perform as a replacement for women,” she explained. And if the designer felt like she was finding her footing in the menswear category during the spring 2025 season, this time felt much more refined. One of the best men’s looks was a brown patterned camp shirt styled with loose satin pants. There was also a matching womenswear look in the same print, a scarf top with fringed detailing at the bottom that felt and looked luxurious, showcasing a new approach for a designer who is best known for sexy going out pieces.</p><p>Like Donatella Versace, Shui is no stranger to a plunging chiffon dress in a colorful print. She even tried her own take on Versace’s JLo dress: a purple butterfly dress with a deep-V and sparkly emblem at the bottom. It comes at a good time, since Amazon Prime’s <em>Off Campus</em> has re-popularized the JLo pop culture moment. While Shui’s version won’t be creating any new search engines or websites, if the retail versions look as good as it did on the runway, it will certainly drive sales.</p></div>",
